WHAT'S AN AUTO SCALING GROUP?
    - An auto scaling group is a free AWS service that automatically launches and terminates EC2 intances to ensure your application always has the right amount of computing 
    capacity, rather then manually adding and removing servers as demand changes an auto scaling group does this automatically based on rules you've define, This helps maintain
    application performance, improve availablilty, and reduce costs by only running number of instances you actually need.

    - WHY DO WE NEED AUTO SCALING
        -  Imagine your website normally receives 1,000 visitors per hour, so two EC2 instances are enough to handle the traffic, During a major sale, traffic suddenly increases to 10,000 visitors per hour. Without Auto Scaling, your existing servers may become overloaded, causing slow response times or even application failures.An Auto Scaling Group detects the increased demand and automatically launches additional EC2 instances to distribute the workload. Once traffic returns to normal, it terminates the extra instances so you're no longer paying for unused capacity.

    - HOW DOES AUTO SCALING GROUP WORK 
        - An Auto Scaling Group continuously monitors your application's performance using metrics from Amazon CloudWatch.

            When a scaling policy is triggered:

                - If demand increases, the ASG launches additional EC2 instances (scale out).
                - If demand decreases, the ASG terminates unnecessary EC2 instances (scale in).

            For example, you could configure a policy that says:

                - If average CPU utilization exceeds 70% for five minutes, launch another EC2 instance.
                - If average CPU utilization falls below 30% for ten minutes, terminate one EC2 instance.

            This allows your infrastructure to automatically adapt to changing workloads.

    -  AUTO SCALING WITH A LOAD BALANCER
        -   Auto Scaling Groups are commonly used together with an Application Load Balancer (ALB).
            example below: 
                                Internet
                                  │
                                  ▼
                         Application Load Balancer
                              │      │      │
                              ▼      ▼      ▼
                          EC2-1  EC2-2  EC2-3
                                     ▲
                                     │
                             Auto Scaling Group

        - The Auto Scaling Group manages the EC2 instances, while the Application Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across all healthy instances.When the ASG launches a new EC2 instance, it is automatically registered with the load balancer. If an instance is terminated, it is automatically deregistered so it no longer receives traffic.

    DESIRED / MINIMUM AND MAXIMUM CAPACITY
        - Every auto scaling group has three important settings:
            * MINIMUM capacity  (The smallest number of EC2 instances that must always be running.)
            * DESIRED capacity  (The number of EC2 instances AWS tries to maintain under normal conditions.)
            * MAXIMUM capacity  (The largest number of EC2 instances the ASG is allowed to launch.)
        
        - Under normal conditions, the ASG keeps 3 instances running. If traffic increases, it can launch up to 8 instances. If demand falls, it won't reduce the number of instances below 2, ensuring the application remains available.

    HEALTH CHECKS 
        -  Auto scaling regularly checks health of EC2 intstances, if an instance crashes, become unreachableor fails health checks. the Auto Scaling Group automatically terminates it and launches a replacement to maintain the desired capacity, This helps keep your application highly available without manual intervention.
        
    LAUNCH TEMPLATE
        - A Launch Template is an AWS resource that acts as a blueprint for launching EC2 instances.Instead of manually specifying the instance configuration every time you create a new EC2 instance, you define the settings once in a Launch Template. Services such as Auto Scaling Groups, EC2, and EC2 Fleet can then use that template to launch identical instances whenever needed, A Launch Template ensures that every EC2 instance is created with the same configuration, making deployments faster, more consistent, and less prone to human error.
    
        WHY DO WE NEED A LAUNCH TEMPLATE?
            - imagine you have a auto scaling group that needs to launch new EC2 instance whenever traffic increases, the auto scaling group knows when to launch a new instance, but it doesnt know how to configure it. 

            - it need sto answer questions such as:

                * Which Amazon Machine Image (AMI) should be used?
                * What EC2 instance type should be launched?
                * Which security group should be attached?
                * Which SSH key pair should be used?
                * Which subnet should the instance be placed in?
                * Should a user data script run when the instance starts?

            the launch template provides all of this information

  
    AUTO SCALING GROUPS AND CLOUD WATCH ALARMS 

        - WHAT ARE CLOUD WATCH ALARMS 
            - A cloudwatch alarm monitors a cloudwatch metric and automatically takes actions, when the metric reaches a threshopld you define,  Think of cloudwatch alarm as the decision-maker for your auto scaling group. It continuouly watchs metric such as cpu utilization, network traffic, or memory usage (if configured), and it tells the auto scaling group when its time to add or remove EC2 instances.


        - WHY DO WE NEED CLOUDWATCH ALARMS?
            - Imagine your application normally runs 2 EC2 instancesand suddenly hundreds of users visit your website at the same time, causing cpu utilization to rise to 85%

            without cloudwatch alarms: 
                * AWS doesnt know that your servers are under heavy load 
                * your application may become slow or unresponsive 
            With cloudwatch alarms:
                * The alarm detects that CPU usage has exceeded the threshold.
                * It triggers a scaling policy.
                * The Auto Scaling Group launches another EC2 instance to handle the increased traffic.

                Users Visit Website
                        │
                        ▼
            EC2 CPU Utilization Increases
                        │
                        ▼
            CloudWatch Collects Metrics
                        │
                        ▼
            CloudWatch Alarm Triggered
                        │
                        ▼
                Scaling Policy Runs
                        │
                        ▼
        Auto Scaling Group Launches New EC2 Instance
                        │
                        ▼
        Load Balancer Begins Sending Traffic

    
        
